 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Construction",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Red uses an AlInGaP die; green and blue use InGaN dice. Each die has its own anode and cathode lead, so the three colours can be wired and driven in any arrangement, and each has a separate thermal path, which lets it run at higher current. The encapsulant is diffused silicone and the terminals are silver plated."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Radiation pattern",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The three colours share nearly the same beam shape along the package X axis, the axis across the lead rows through the middle die. Along the Y axis, the line of the three dice, the peaks are spread: red near +10°, green near 0° and blue near -10°."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal derating",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Maximum DC current is flat to a knee and then falls linearly to 100 °C. With one die lit, red holds 50 mA to about 74 °C solder point or 52 °C ambient; green and blue hold 25 mA to about 88 °C solder point or 72 °C ambient. With all three lit the knees move down: red to about 67 °C solder point or 31 °C ambient, green and blue to about 77 °C or 46 °C. Junction-to-solder-point and junction-to-ambient resistance both rise when all three dice are on."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Drive",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Constant-current drive is recommended for consistent output. Keep current within the derating curves at every temperature. The dice are not for reverse bias; in a matrix the reverse voltage across any die must stay within its rating."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Binning",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Each reel holds parts from one intensity bin and one colour bin. For this part number the intensity spans three bins up from the minimum for each colour, and colour spans green bins A to C and blue bins A to D; red has a single wavelength bin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Enlarge the copper of pins 1, 4 and 5 to spread heat. The land pattern is two rows of three 0.50 × 1.60 mm pads, 2.30 mm across each row with 0.40 mm gaps, the rows 1.35 mm apart and 4.55 mm overall."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Assembly",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Solder by reflow, with the lead rows lying along the reflow direction. Put no force on the part during reflow or while it is still hot. Hand soldering is for rework only, within the hand-soldering limits, without the iron touching the body; desolder with a double flat tip."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Handling silicone",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The silicone lens is soft. Pick parts up by the body only; never press on or pierce the lens with tweezers or other sharp tools, and do not stack assembled boards. Dust on the lens comes off with a cotton bud and isopropyl alcohol, rubbed lightly; ultrasonic cleaning is not advised. A 1.7 mm inner, 3.5 mm outer nozzle has been tested for pick-and-place."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Environment",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Sulfur compounds and corrosive gases degrade the optics and life of the part; sources include rubber gaskets, RTV silicone rubber and rubber gloves. Sudden ambient temperature changes in humid air cause condensation. The part rates IPX6 under IEC 60529:2001, tested on a potted board; outdoor use still needs protection from water, dust, oil, gases and mechanical stress. Unused parts stay sealed in their moisture-barrier packing, since the silver leads tarnish in air and solder poorly."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD and eye safety",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The LED is ESD class 1C and needs ESD-safe handling. Do not look straight into a lit part; use shielding or eye protection."
  }
 ],
